    `js_optical_flow_dense.html`
  All four C++ functions exist and are exported (`CV_EXPORTS_W`) in the
  local OpenCV 5.0.0 checkout's `opencv2/video/tracking.hpp` - this is
  pure binding work, not a missing dependency. A new `js_video.cpp`
  (mirroring the existing `js_calib3d.cpp`/`js_dnn.cpp` module-file
  pattern) would cover all four in one place.

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      cv.CamShift, cv.meanShift, cv.calcOpticalFlowPyrLK, cv.calcOpticalFlowFarneback
      // all undefined
    });

  Correction (2026-09-05): `cv.CamShift`/`cv.meanShift` were already bound
  (`js_imgproc.cpp`'s `js_imgproc_track()`, which already includes
  `opencv2/video/tracking.hpp`) - this entry's premise that the whole
  category was unbound was stale by the time this was investigated; only
  the two optical-flow functions were actually missing. Fixed those two:
  added `js_cv_calc_optical_flow_pyr_lk()` and
  `js_cv_calc_optical_flow_farneback()` to `js_imgproc.cpp` (same file,
  no new `js_video.cpp` needed since the include was already there).
  Tests: tests/unittests/test_segmentation_video.js.

- wunused-function-cant-see-header-only-static-inline: Neither GCC's nor
  Clang's `-Wunused-function` can find dead `static inline` functions in a
  header like `include/jsbindings.hpp` (every one of its 82 free functions
  is `static inline`; it has zero plain-`static` ones - confirmed via
  `grep -nE '^static [A-Za-z_<:].*$' include/jsbindings.hpp | grep -v
  'static inline'`, no matches). Two independent, compiler-verified
  reasons:
    1. GCC never warns for `static inline`, only plain `static` - confirmed
       on g++ 13.3.0 and 16.0.1 (trunk): `static inline int f(){}` vs
       `static int g(){}`, neither called, only `g` warns (with or without
       `-fsyntax-only`).
    2. Clang *does* warn for an unused `static inline` function, but only
       when it's declared directly in the main translation unit being
       compiled - never for one reached via `#include`, regardless of
       inline-ness otherwise. Confirmed with a 2x2 repro (static /
       static-inline x header / main-file): header-declared `static`
       warns, header-declared `static inline` doesn't; main-file-declared
       `static` and `static inline` both warn. An earlier note in this
       file claimed "Clang's -Wunused-function does fire for static
       inline (verified)" - that verification was against a main-file-only
       repro and doesn't hold for the header-inclusion case that actually
       matters for `jsbindings.hpp`, corrected here.
  Net effect: an aggregated `-Wunused-function` sweep across all 54 `.cpp`
  files that include `jsbindings.hpp` (now working end-to-end after
  clang-thread-local-nontrivial-dtor-inputoutputarray was fixed) ran
  clean under Clang with zero compile errors, but also produced zero
  findings - not because nothing is dead, but because nothing in the
  header was ever eligible to be flagged in the first place. Confirmed
  the flag genuinely fires in this exact build's command line (not
  silently swallowed by some other flag) by injecting a deliberately
  unused `static inline` canary via `-include`: still silent, consistent
  with reason 2 above (force-included files behave like `#include`, not
  like main-file content). This closes off `-Wunused-function` entirely
  as a technique for this header, under either compiler.

- freetype-puttext-y-ignores-gettextsize-ascent: For a *scalable* (non
  bitmap-strike) font, `cv::freetype::FreeType2::putText()`'s `point.y`
  does not mean "top of the ink bbox that `getTextSize()`'s reported
  ascent/height describes", contrary to this project's own doc comments
  (`examples/render_font.js`'s "Draw.text's point is the glyph box's
  top-left corner, not its baseline"). Found while building a small
  single-string terminal preview renderer in `examples/render_font.js`
  sized tightly to `ascent+descent` from `TextStyle.size()` - it rendered
  entirely blank for characters that are provably non-empty (verified they
  draw fine on a taller canvas).

  Measured the actual offset between `point.y` and where ink actually
  lands (draw at `point=(0, py)` on a generously tall canvas, scan for the
  ink row range, subtract `py`) across 3 fonts x 4 sizes:

    monogram.ttf        8px: ink at point+[4,7]     14px: point+[7,13]    24px: point+[13,23]   40px: point+[22,39]
    Pixeletter.ttf       8px: ink at point+[1,7]      14px: point+[3,13]    24px: point+[5,23]    40px: point+[8,39]
    DejaVuSansMono.ttf   8px: ink at point+[2,7]      14px: point+[4,13]    24px: point+[6,23]    40px: point+[11,39]

  In every case the ink's top edge lands roughly `0.15-0.6 * fontSize`
  *below* `point.y` (never above), and its bottom edge lands almost
  exactly at `point.y + fontSize - 1` - i.e. `point.y` behaves like "top of
  a full em-square line box", not "top of the measured glyph ink" the way
  `size()`'s `ascent` (`height` field) would imply if you tried to size a
  canvas as `ascent + descent` and draw at `y = ascent - glyphAscent` the
  way this project's own `render_font.js` grid exporter does. `size()`'s
  `.width` is unaffected - only vertical placement is wrong relative to
  what `.height`/the baseline callback claim.

  **Confirmed knock-on bug in the existing PNG/JSON grid exporter**
  (`examples/render_font.js`'s `exportFontSheet`, unchanged by this
  session, predates it): every glyph placed in **row 0** of the grid (the
  common case - it's whichever codepoints sort first, typically space and
  punctuation) silently renders nothing, because `y = row*cellHeight +
  (ascent - glyphAscent)` puts row-0 glyphs right at the canvas top with no
  headroom for this offset, and the top of a `Mat.zeros()` canvas simply
  clips ink that would land above y=0 if this offset were even larger, or
  in row 0's case, renders correctly-if-you-are-lucky since the offset here
  is instead well *below* row 0's y - meaning the row-0 glyph is drawn far
  enough down that it bleeds into row 1's cell rather than clipping, and
  what actually shows up empty is row 0's expected cell position, not row 1
  itself. Verified via `qjsm examples/render_font.js monogram.ttf
  --end=0x7f` then checking pixel data at the JSON-reported cell for a
  row-0 character (`!`, U+21): zero ink in that cell despite `!` being a
  perfectly ordinary non-empty glyph.

  Worked around (not fixed - this is upstream, same file/line as the two
  bugs above) in the new terminal preview renderer added this session
  (`TerminalGlyphRenderer` in `examples/render_font.js`): draws into a
  generously tall scratch canvas (`fontSize*2 + 4`) at `point.y=0`, then
  scans for the actual ink row range and crops to that before rendering -
  sidesteps trusting `point.y` placement math entirely, at the cost of a
  bit of wasted canvas per render. The grid exporter's row-0 bug is left
  as-is per this project's "surgical changes" convention (not what this
  session was asked to fix); worth doing the same generous-canvas-then-crop
  treatment there if row-0 export fidelity ever matters.

    import { TextStyle } from './js/cvHighGUI.js';
    import { CV_8UC1, LINE_AA, Mat, Point } from 'opencv';
    const style = new TextStyle('/home/roman/Downloads/fonts/monogram.ttf', 16);
    const mat = Mat.zeros(8, 7, CV_8UC1); // sized to size('A').height (8) - looks reasonable, isn't
    style.draw(mat, 'A', new Point(0, 0), 255, -1, LINE_AA);
    console.log([...mat.data].filter(v => v != 0).length); // 0 - nothing drawn, despite 'A' being non-empty

- render_font-single-strike-assumption: `render_font.js`'s `queryFontMetadata()`
  only captures the FIRST `pixelsize` value fc-query prints
  (`/^pixelsize=(\d+...)/m`, no `/g`), and `findCrispSize()`/the detail
  view built on it accordingly only ever report ONE embedded-bitmap-strike
  size as "the" crisp size. But the bitmap-strike table formats
  (`EBLC`/`CBLC`/older `bloc`) are explicitly designed to hold a `strikes`
  array - a single font FILE can legitimately bundle several distinct
  fixed pixel sizes (e.g. 8/10/12/16px), each a genuine 1-bit/grayscale
  bitmap, not a scaled copy. Confirmed the tables/API exist and are
  queryable via fontTools on this machine (`NotoColorEmoji.ttf`'s `CBLC`
  table, `TTFont(f)['CBLC'].strikes[i].bitmapSizeTable.ppemX/ppemY`) -
  just didn't happen to find a genuinely multi-strike (>1 entry) file
  installed locally to verify the multi-size case end-to-end against.
  Not fixed this session (discovered while answering a direct question,
  out of scope for the color-mode work in progress) - `queryFontProgramInfo()`
  (already shells out to fontTools for other program-level facts) is the
  natural place to add an `EBLC`/`CBLC`/`bloc` strikes list, and
  `findCrispSize()` would need to report/offer all of them rather than
  assuming one.

    python3 -c "
    from fontTools.ttLib import TTFont
    t = TTFont('/usr/share/fonts/truetype/noto/NotoColorEmoji.ttf', lazy=True)
    for strike in t['CBLC'].strikes:
        h = strike.bitmapSizeTable
        print(h.ppemX, h.ppemY, h.bitDepth)
    "
    # -> one strike here (109 109 32) - single-strike on this machine, but
    # the `strikes` list itself is exactly where a multi-strike font's
    # sizes would show up if one were installed.

- opencv-freetype-fontscale-truncated-to-int: `TextStyle`'s `fontScale`
  (`js/cvHighGUI.js`) is passed through `js_draw.cpp` to
  `cv::freetype::FreeType2::putText()`/`getTextSize()` as a genuine
  `double`, unchanged and untruncated by this project's own binding code
  (`js_draw.cpp:596-704` - `font_scale` stays a `double` the whole way
  through). Found while adding fine-grained (0.5px) font-size stepping to
  `render_font.js`'s glyph-map view: `TextStyle('font.ttf', 8.5).size('A')`
  measures bit-for-bit identical to `size 8` - in fact `8, 8.5, 9, 9.5, 10`
  all measured identical for `monogram.ttf`. Since this project's own code
  doesn't truncate it, the rounding must happen inside
  `opencv_contrib/modules/freetype/src/freetype.cpp` itself (almost
  certainly an internal `FT_Set_Pixel_Sizes(face, (int)fontScale,
  (int)fontScale)` - that FreeType call only accepts integer `FT_UInt`
  pixel sizes; a fractional pixel size would need `FT_Set_Char_Size`'s
  26.6 fixed-point sizing instead, which needs a DPI figure and isn't
  what this call is using) - no local source checkout to confirm the
  exact line (see other freetype.cpp findings above, same situation).
  Practical effect: any fractional `--size`/`fontScale` silently behaves
  like `Math.floor()`'d to the next integer down, with no error or
  signal that the fractional part was ignored - and even *adjacent
  integers* sometimes measure identical at these tiny sizes (`8`, `9`,
  `10` all gave the same `4x5` for `monogram.ttf`), which is FreeType's
  own internal pixel-grid rounding at very small sizes, not part of this
  particular bug. Not worked around - `render_font.js`'s new fine-size
  stepping still tracks/displays the requested fractional size (so
  intermediate values aren't lost if the underlying rendering eventually
  supports them), it just means some 0.5px steps currently look identical
  to their neighbor on screen.

    import { TextStyle } from './js/cvHighGUI.js';
    const f = '/home/roman/Downloads/fonts/monogram.ttf';
    for (const size of [8, 8.5, 9, 9.5, 10]) {
      const s = new TextStyle(f, size).size('A');
      console.log(size, '->', s.width, s.height);
    }
    // all five print the same "4 5" - the .5 (and the 8->9->10 step) has no effect

- onnx-importer-rejects-dynamic-shape-graphs: OpenCV 5.0.0's builtin `cv.readNetFromONNX` fails to load any ONNX graph
  exported with HuggingFace/optimum's default dynamic `batch_size`/`sequence_length` axes (same failure on an XLM-R-style
  model and LaBSE, at the `onnx_node!/embeddings/Shape` node: "dynamic 'zero' shapes are not supported"). Upstream
  limitation of the importer. Workaround: re-export with static shapes (`examples/models/setup-embedding-model.py`), or use
  ONNX Runtime: `cv.readNetFromONNX(path, cv.dnn.ENGINE_ORT)` on an OpenCV built with WITH_ONNXRUNTIME=ON (see
  doc/dnn-engines.md). STILL TO VERIFY: loading such a graph through ENGINE_ORT, needs an ORT-enabled OpenCV build (none
  installed on the dev host; build-opencv.sh now fetches ORT for every target).


- PARTIALLY FIXED: mat-mul-is-elementwise-not-matrix-multiplication: `Mat.mul(other)`
  (magic `MAT_EXPR_MUL` in `js_mat.cpp`) calls `cv::Mat::mul()`, which is an
  elementwise (Hadamard) product requiring both operands to have identical
  rows/cols/type/channels - it throws "Mat dimensions mismatch" for a real
  (n x k)*(k x m) multiply, despite the name suggesting matrix
  multiplication. Checked against the real opencv.js (Mat.d.ts,
  core_array.d.ts from the @techstark/opencv-js npm package, not just the
  minified WASM glue, which doesn't keep method names as greppable text):
  opencv.js has no `Mat.matMul()` either - its own `Mat.mul()` is the same
  elementwise op, and real matrix multiplication is only available via the
  free function `cv.gemm(src1, src2, alpha, src3, beta, dst, flags?)`.
  `cv.gemm` is now implemented here too (`OTHER_GEMM` in `js_cv.cpp`),
  matching that real signature exactly - so this is fixed for anyone calling
  `cv.gemm` directly. Still open: `Mat.mul()`'s name remains misleading for
  anyone who doesn't know to reach for `cv.gemm` instead. Before `cv.gemm`
  existed, `mediathek-semantic.js`'s `rankByCosine()` (parent repo) batched a
  dot product against many rows via `cv.repeat` + `cv.multiply` +
  `cv.reduce(..., 0 /* REDUCE_SUM */)`; that workaround still works but
  `cv.gemm` is the more direct fix now.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    const cand = new cv.Mat(3, 4, cv.CV_32F, Float32Array.from([1,0,0,0, 0,1,0,0, 0.6,0.8,0,0]).buffer);
    const q = new cv.Mat(4, 1, cv.CV_32F, Float32Array.from([1,0,0,0]).buffer);
    cand.mul(q); // throws: Mat dimensions mismatch (3x4 vs 4x1)

- js-cv-math-no-scalar-operand: `compare()`/`absdiff()`/`add()`/`divide()`/
  `max()`/`min()`/`multiply()`/`subtract()` (all JS_CFUNC_MAGIC_DEF'd onto
  `js_cv_math()` in js_cv.cpp) resolve *both* operands via
  `js_cv_inputoutputarray()`, which (unlike `js_cv_inputarray()`) has no
  plain-JS-array/number-to-cv::Scalar fallback - only Mat/UMat/JSVector/
  Line/ArrayBuffer/TypedArray convert. Passing a bare number or plain array
  as the second operand (e.g. `compare(idxMat, 3, mask, CMP_EQ)` to test an
  index Mat against a constant) silently resolves that operand to
  `cv::noArray()`, and `cv::compare()` then throws
  `(-215:Assertion failed) _src1.empty() == _src2.empty()` since one side is
  empty and the other isn't - found while fixing
  js/vectorizer/vector/methods/palette-regions.js, which needed to test a
  CV_8U index map against a per-band integer constant. `inRange(src,
  lowerb, upperb, dst)` (js_cv_other/OTHER_IN_RANGE) DOES accept a
  plain array for its Scalar bounds (via `js_cv_inputarray()`, which has the
  array-to-cv::Scalar branch) and was used as the workaround:
  `inRange(idx, [k,k,k,k], [k,k,k,k], mask)` instead of
  `compare(idx, k, mask, CMP_EQ)`.

    import { imread, Mat, compare, CMP_EQ } from 'opencv';
    const mat = imread('some.png');
    const mask = new Mat();
    compare(mat, 128, mask, CMP_EQ); // throws: _src1.empty() == _src2.empty()

    import { Mat, Size } from 'opencv';
    const m = new Mat(new Size(10, 10), 16); // meant to be CV_8UC3
    console.log(m.channels(), m.depth()); // 1, 16 - not 3, 8

- highgui-gtk-getwindowproperty-unimplemented: `cv::getWindowProperty(name,
  prop_id)` (js_highgui.cpp:js_cv_get_window_property) always returns -1 for
  EVERY prop_id on this build's GTK backend, even `WND_PROP_VISIBLE` queried
  against a window that was just created and is actively being drawn to -
  `window_gtk.cpp` has zero `getWindowProperty`-related code, so
  `CvWindow::getProperty()` falls through to NaN for any prop_id, which
  `cv::getWindowProperty` (window.cpp) converts to -1. This makes -1
  useless as an "is the window still open" signal on GTK: it's returned
  whether the window is open, closed, or the property is simply
  unsupported. Found while trying to detect the WM's X (close) button in
  js/vectorizer/gui/app.js's run loop - the first attempt polled
  `getWindowProperty(WIN, WND_PROP_VISIBLE) < 1` once per frame, which is
  always true, so the app tore its own window down after exactly one loop
  iteration (looked like "the GUI won't show up" from outside). The working
  substitute is `cv::getWindowImageRect()`/`getWindowImageRect(name)`:
  GTK's `cvGetWindowRect_GTK` throws `cv::Error::StsNullPtr` ("NULL
  window") once `icvOnClose`/`icvDeleteWindow_` has torn the window down,
  which is a real, catchable signal - poll it once per frame in a
  try/catch instead.

    import { namedWindow, getWindowProperty, getWindowImageRect,
             WINDOW_AUTOSIZE, WND_PROP_VISIBLE } from 'opencv';
    namedWindow('w', WINDOW_AUTOSIZE);
    console.log(getWindowProperty('w', WND_PROP_VISIBLE)); // -1, window IS open
    // after the user clicks the titlebar X:
    try { getWindowImageRect('w'); } catch (e) { /* now it throws - window is gone */ }

- NOT a qjs-opencv bug - os.Worker message delivery is broken in the
