  of anything in this repo's own JS/C++ code.

    cd /mnt/data/Projects/plot-cv/quickjs/tests
    qjsm test_worker.js   # hangs forever, no output, on the installed qjsm

- cvchannel-getter-only-classes-lost-in-encode: `js/cvChannel.js`'s
  `encode()` walks plain objects via `Object.keys(value)` to recurse into
  RPC payloads sent through SharedChannel/os.Worker. `cv.Size` (and
  presumably `cv.Rect`/`cv.Point`/other geometry classes) expose their
  fields as getters on the prototype, not as own enumerable properties -
  confirmed `Object.keys(new cv.Size(5,5))` returns `[]` and
  `JSON.stringify(new cv.Size(5,5))` returns `'{}'` even though
  `.width`/`.height` read correctly. So passing a `new cv.Size(...)` (or
  similar) as an argument through `ThreadPool.run()`/`SharedChannel` is
  silently corrupted into `{}` on the other side, which then fails
  wherever the receiving cv.* binding requires a valid ksize/size arg
  (hit this via `GaussianBlur(gray, blurred, new Size(5,5), 0)` in a
  worker - failed with an opaque error). Worked around in
  tests/test_threadpool.js by passing a plain `[w, h]` array instead
  (cv's own arg parsing, e.g. js_size.hpp's `js_size_read`, already
  accepts arrays or plain objects, not just Size instances) rather than
  fixing encode() itself.

    import { encode } from '../js/cvChannel.js';
    import { Size } from 'opencv';
    console.log(encode(new Size(5, 5)));   // {} - width/height lost

- qjsm-worker-self-reference-drops-messages: on the qjsm build used in
  this project, `new os.Worker(import.meta.url)` (the same file sourced
  as both the main-thread module and the worker's own entry module)
  silently drops every worker -> parent `postMessage()` - the worker
  side runs fine (imports resolve, `onmessage` fires, its own
  `postMessage()` calls return normally) but the main thread's
  `worker.onmessage` never fires, even with extended polling via
  `os.sleepAsync`. Confirmed this is specific to same-file
  self-reference, not a general worker -> parent pipe bug: an identical
  setup using two separate files (one importing/constructing the
  Worker, a sibling file as its entry point) delivers worker -> parent
  messages correctly via the same `Worker.onmessage` API. This matches
  pre-existing comments already in tests/test_worker.js and
  tests/test_worker_body.js ("qjs os.Worker drops worker->parent
  messages when the same file is used for both branches"). Root cause
  not isolated further (likely in qjsm.c's or quickjs-libc.c's
  worker/module bootstrap, given the two related `jsm_context_new()`
  bugs already found and fixed upstream in
  ../quickjs/qjs-modules/src/qjsm.c - missing `JS_SetModuleLoaderFunc`
  and `init_list_head(&loaded_modules)` for worker runtimes). Worked
  around throughout this project (js/cvThreadPool.js +
  js/cvThreadPoolWorker.js, tests/test_worker.js +
  tests/test_worker_body.js) by always using two separate files instead
  of the self-referencing `import.meta.url` pattern.

    # worker_self.js - sourced as both branches, worker -> parent silently dropped
    import * as os from 'os';
    if (os.Worker.parent) {
      os.Worker.parent.onmessage = (e) => os.Worker.parent.postMessage('pong');
    } else {
      const w = new os.Worker(import.meta.url);
      w.onmessage = (e) => console.log('never printed:', e.data);
      w.postMessage('ping');
    }

- umat-empty-continuous-not-methods: `js_umat.cpp`'s `.empty`/`.continuous`
  are getters (`JS_CGETSET_MAGIC_DEF`, PROP_EMPTY/PROP_CONTINUOUS at
  js_umat.cpp:1026,1029), the exact same discrepancy vs. opencv.js
  (`mat.empty()`/`mat.isContinuous()` are methods there) that js_mat.cpp's
  Mat class had before this session's fix. Not fixed here - only Mat was
  in scope for this pass - but should get the identical treatment
  (JS_CFUNC_MAGIC_DEF methods named `empty`/`isContinuous`) plus a sweep
  of every JS caller in this repo currently reading `.empty`/`.continuous`
  on a UMat instance as a bare property.

[RETRACTED 2026-09-07: not a real bug. The mask used in the original repro
was built via `cv.drawContours(mask, [contour], -1, ...)` with a plain JS
array - i.e. it hit the drawcontours-silently-noops-on-plain-array bug
below, which left `mask` silently all-zero. minMaxLoc's `{0, 0, (-1,-1),
(-1,-1)}` result was the documented-correct answer for an all-zero mask,
not a minMaxLoc defect. Verified with a mask built via cv.rectangle instead
(known-good drawing path): minMaxLoc respects the mask correctly, including
rejecting an extremum placed outside it. No entry needed - keeping this
note only so a future session doesn't re-suspect minMaxLoc from a
similar-looking symptom without checking how its mask was built first.]

- vectorizer-stage3-worker-pool-dispatch-never-received (UNRESOLVED, worker
  pool now disabled): despite everything in
  vectorizer-stage3-wired-to-worker-pool-for-real-parallelism above being
  genuinely fixed and verified, a live GUI session still hung on a clean,
  single-frame, no-navigation vectorize call. Diagnostic logging added
  directly to js/cvThreadPool.js and js/vectorizer/vector/poolWorker.js
  showed: all 4 pool workers boot and report ready at App construction
  time; PoolWorker.run() logs `dispatching id=1 method=canny` (i.e.
  chan.postMessage() was called on the main thread); and then nothing -
  no `onmessage id=1` is ever logged on the worker side, even after 8+
  seconds. A structurally different earlier repro (which involved
  navigating between frames, confounding it with the separate,
  since-fixed cross-frame race - see stage3-process.js's git history)
  showed the opposite: the worker completed and posted a reply that the
  main thread never logged receiving. Both point at message loss
  somewhere in the os.Worker/SharedChannel transport, not in this
  project's own JS logic - a standalone headless script
  (tests/vectorizer_pool_cli.js) driving the exact same ThreadPool code
  path has never once reproduced this, every run resolving in well under a
  second. Leading unconfirmed hypothesis (see vectorizer-debug.md §3.1):
  workers sit idle for real user-navigation time (seconds to tens of
  seconds) before their first job in the GUI, vs. milliseconds in the CLI
  script, and something about that idle period breaks the pipe. Not
  root-caused this session - ran out of time/attempts. Action taken
  instead of chasing this further live: stage3-process.js's _run() no
  longer uses app.vectorPool at all - it now calls
  VectorMethod.apply(frame.mat, params, meta) directly, synchronously, on
  the GUI thread (see vectorizer-pipeline-run-in-process-not-worker-pool
  below). js/cvThreadPool.js and js/vectorizer/vector/poolWorker.js are
  left in the tree, unused, for whoever picks this back up -
  vectorizer-debug.md's §5 has the ranked next steps (env-gated
  permanent logging, an idle-delay repro via a CLI flag, a payload-free
  ping test, qjsm-debugger single-stepping).

- vectorizer-pipeline-run-in-process-not-worker-pool: pivot away from
  vectorizer-stage3-worker-pool-dispatch-never-received above, done
  instead of continuing to chase that hang live. Two changes: (1) new
  js/vectorizer/vectorizer-repl.js - a REPL-based, non-GUI client (using
  the `repl` module, same one cv-shell.js uses) exposing load()/use()/
  set()/go()/svg() etc. as globals over the real Model/Pipeline/Loader/
  Registry/Composer, calling Pipeline.process() -> VectorMethod.apply()
  directly with no worker/thread involved at all. Verified: all 10
  registered methods run successfully against tests/box.png (9ms-2045ms
  each), 5/5 identical-output repeat runs (no flakiness), and a full
  load->use->go->svg round trip against a second image
  (tests/opencv-logo.png) writes a valid SVG. (2)
  js/vectorizer/gui/stages/stage3-process.js's _run() rewritten the same
  way: no more app.vectorPool.runLatest(), just a direct, synchronous
  method.apply() call. This also let the per-frame supersession/race
  guard (isCurrent() checks, the Superseded import) be deleted outright -
  with the call now synchronous end-to-end, there is never a second
  _run() in flight to race against. js/vectorizer/gui/app.js no longer
  constructs a ThreadPool at all. Trade-off, explicit: a slow method now
  blocks app.run()'s frame loop (the window stops redrawing/responding to
  input) for the call's duration instead of running off-thread - with
  cv/loader.js's load-time downscale to 1600px already in place, that's
  under ~2s for every method tested so far on a small image, but has NOT
  yet been verified against the large real photos that originally
  motivated real parallelism (see vectorizer-debug.md). Not yet re-tested
  against a live GUI session (needs the user's own hands - see
  vectorizer-debug.md's constraints on unreliable xdotool-driven GUI
  testing in this sandbox); the REPL is the verified-tonight artifact,
  the GUI rewrite is the same code path but not independently re-run
  live.

- segment-no-subject-small-object: segment fails with E_METHOD "the model found no subject above --min-area" on a small object tapped in its middle (AIM-500 image 031, object 1% of the photo, tap at its thickest point), so the editor shows nothing. Not fixed: look at --min-area 0.0002 against the tier (preview) size and at which candidate the check runs on.

    opencv-imgproc segment --in 031.jpg --units norm --point 0.454,0.703,1 --out cut.png --candidates 3

- find-quickjs-fatal-without-installed-quickjs (cmake/FindQuickJS.cmake:140, find_quickjs()): without an
  installed quickjs the macro prints the default paths as if they existed, then pkg_search_module(
  QUICKJS REQUIRED quickjs) stops the configure ("None of the required 'quickjs' found",
  CMakeLists.txt:49 and :104). No BuildQuickJS.cmake, older FindQuickJS.cmake. "install directory:"
  prints empty and the module search path has the triple directory
  lib/aarch64-linux-android/aarch64-unknown-linux-android30/quickjs. Found 2026-11-15 on a fresh
  Termux. Fix: adopt the canonical FindQuickJS.cmake and BuildQuickJS.cmake.

    cmake -S . -B build/x   # no quickjs installed

- lccv-hardcoded-include (LCCV/src/lccv.cpp:2): includes <libcamera/libcamera/stream.h>, a path that only
  resolves when <prefix>/include is a compiler search path (system install); with libcamera in another
  prefix it also finds the system copy and fails with "redefinition of class libcamera::StreamFormats".
  Worked around in cmake/libcamera.cmake (adds <prefix>/include); the real fix is <libcamera/stream.h> in
  the LCCV submodule. Found 2026-11-20 building against BUILD_LIBCAMERA=ON.

- aruco-calib3d-lists-are-js-arrays-not-matvector (js_aruco.cpp, js_calib3d.cpp, js_fisheye.cpp): per-view and per-marker
  lists (aruco corners/rejected, calibrateCamera objectPoints/imagePoints/rvecs/tvecs, ...) are plain JS arrays of Mat
  (include/js_matvec.hpp), not opencv.js MatVector objects, until phase 2 (see no-opencvjs-matvector). Code written for
  opencv.js that calls corners.get(i) or corners.size() does not work yet.
